<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Gradle dependency verification (docs/architecture/release.md#gradle:
  "Dependency verification metadata committed. CI uses strict verification.").

  This is the policy TEMPLATE, not an active verification file.

  It is deliberately NOT named `verification-metadata.xml`: Gradle activates
  dependency verification purely on that filename's presence, so committing it
  with an empty <components/> block would fail every single resolution with
  "Dependency verification failed" rather than verifying anything.

  This file holds the *policy*, which is a human decision and belongs in
  review. The per-component checksum entries are generated by resolving the
  graph once against google() and mavenCentral():

      ./gradlew [dash][dash]write-verification-metadata sha256 \
        assembleDebug testDebugUnitTest lintDebug :macrobenchmark:assembleBenchmark

  (XML comments cannot contain a double hyphen, so the flag is written above
  with [dash][dash] standing in for the two leading hyphens.)

  Run that command (CI does this automatically while the repository is not yet
  bootstrapped), review the generated <components> block in the diff exactly as
  you would review a lockfile, and commit the result as
  `gradle/verification-metadata.xml`. Verification becomes active from that
  commit onward. CI then runs with the
  verify-dependencies flag, so an artifact whose checksum does not match is
  rejected rather than used. See docs/BUILD.md.

  Policy choices recorded here:
    * verify-metadata: module metadata (POM/Gradle module files) is verified as
      well as artifacts, so a tampered POM cannot pull a substitute artifact.
    * verify-signatures: false for now. PGP signature coverage across the
      Android/AndroidX artifact set is uneven, and turning it on without first
      validating that coverage would be exactly the unvalidated-provider-fact
      failure docs/architecture/README.md warns about. SHA-256 checksum
      verification of every artifact is the enforced floor; signature
      verification is revisited as a reviewed change once coverage is
      established.
    * No trusted-artifact wildcards: nothing is exempted from verification.
-->
<verification-metadata xmlns="https://schema.gradle.org/dependency-verification"
                       xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                       xsi:schemaLocation="https://schema.gradle.org/dependency-verification https://schema.gradle.org/dependency-verification/dependency-verification-1.3.xsd">
   <configuration>
      <verify-metadata>true</verify-metadata>
      <verify-signatures>false</verify-signatures>
   </configuration>
   <components/>
</verification-metadata>
